{"documentId":"tos.work.sextus-empiricus.outlines-of-pyrrhonism","versionId":"retained-outlines-en-forage2026-ai","revision":"5796826c1e9b123b1253409ad3056e735c353f4f4bf94b1bdf84be7251587ff7","units":[{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-abca700b4c094cfab909b62157b46ba7","ordinal":641,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.141","text":"\n\ntime, it is either limited or unlimited. If it is limited, it began from some time and will end at some time. For this reason there was once a time when there was no time (before it began), and there will one day be a time when there will be no time (after it ends), which is absurd. So time is not limited."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-77c5da0a0e794c55bd70bb49b62cc80b","ordinal":642,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.142","text":"\n\nAnd if it is unlimited, then, since part of it is called past, part present and part future, the future and the past either exist or do not. If they do not exist, only the present is left, which is momentary, so time will be limited and the difficulties raised at the start will follow. And if the past exists and the future exists, each of them will be present. But it is absurd to call past and future time present. So time is not unlimited either. And if it is neither unlimited nor limited, time does not exist at all."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-2172325bcc5f4154835578b8faec7a9e","ordinal":643,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.143","text":"\n\nFurther, if time exists, it is either divisible or indivisible. It is not indivisible, since it is divided into present, past and future, as they themselves say. Nor is it divisible. For every divisible thing is measured out by some part of itself, the measuring part being laid against each part of what is measured, as when we measure a cubit with a finger’s breadth. But time cannot be measured out by any part of itself. For if the present, say, measures the past, it will be laid against the past and therefore be past; and in the same way, with the future, it will be future. And if the future were to measure the others, it will be present and past; and the past, similarly, will be future and present. That is incoherent. So time is not divisible either. And if it is neither indivisible nor divisible, it does not exist."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-39071d7a6723485ba083f4e633a27c41","ordinal":644,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.144","text":"\n\nAgain, time is said to have three parts: one past, one present and one future. Of these, past and future do not exist; for if past and future time existed now, each of them would be present. Nor does the present exist. For if present time exists, it is either indivisible or divisible. It is not indivisible, since things that change are said to change in present time, but nothing changes in a partless time, as iron does when it becomes soft, or as each of the other things does. So present time is not indivisible."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-1caaf980933b4227abe3d090276e0cbe","ordinal":645,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.145","text":"\n\nNor is it divisible. It could not be divided into presents, since, because of the swift flux of things in the cosmos, the present is said to change into the past faster than can be conceived. Nor could it be divided into past and future, for then it would have no existence, having one part of itself that no longer exists and another that does not yet exist."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-a4530e8918844bb7acbcd99c7377f006","ordinal":646,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.146","text":"\n\nHence the present cannot be the end of the past and the beginning of the future either, since it will both be and not be: it will be, [then,] as present, but it will not be, since its parts do not exist. So it is not divisible. And if the present is neither indivisible nor divisible, it does not exist. And if neither the present nor the past nor the future exists, time is not anything either; for what is made up of non-existent things is non-existent."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-2324a9fd182747908654430de96094b1","ordinal":647,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.147","text":"\n\nThis argument is also advanced against time. If there is time, it is either generated and perishable or ungenerated and imperishable. It is not ungenerated and imperishable, if part of it is said to be past and no longer to exist, and part to be future and not yet to exist."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-a1cae9b8200e4a4e8389d331c35dba02","ordinal":648,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.148","text":"\n\nNor is it generated and perishable. For, according to the dogmatists’ own hypotheses, what comes into being must come into being from something that exists, and what perishes must perish into something that exists. If, then, it perishes into the past, it perishes into what does not exist; and if it comes into being from the future, it comes into being from what does not exist, for neither of these exists. But it is absurd to say that anything comes into being from what does not exist or perishes into what does not exist. So time is not generated and perishable. And if it is neither ungenerated and imperishable nor generated and perishable, it does not exist at all."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-cd463e2230614c74ba3dddbe135cebdc","ordinal":649,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.149","text":"\n\nFurther, everything that comes into being seems to come into being in time. So if time comes into being, it comes into being in time. Either, then, it comes into being itself in itself, or one time in another. If it comes into being itself in itself, the same thing will both be and not be. For since that in which something comes into being must exist before what comes into being in it, the time that comes into being in itself does not yet exist insofar as it comes into being, but already exists insofar as it comes into being in itself. So it does not come into being in itself."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-42afc2ed347d4016910715f1b5bb97c1","ordinal":650,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.150","text":"\n\nNor does one time come into being in another. For if the present comes into being in the future, the present will be future; and if in the past, it will be past. The same must be said about the other times too. So one time does not come into being in another time either. And if time comes into being neither itself in itself nor one in another, time is not generated. But it was shown that it is not ungenerated either. Being neither generated nor ungenerated, then, it does not exist at all; for each existing thing must be either generated or ungenerated."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-436d01ba9d7945e19a8052356329d2fb","ordinal":651,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.151","text":"\n\nSince time seems not to be observed without number, it would not be out of place to go briefly through number as well. As far as everyday practice goes, we say, without holding beliefs, that we count things, and we hear that number is something. But the dogmatists’ over-subtlety has stirred up the argument against this too."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-39fa174b97004b8b93cd3ac0a5c3f14f","ordinal":652,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.152","text":"\n\nThe Pythagoreans, for example, say that numbers are actually the elements of the cosmos. They say that what appears is composed of something, and that elements must be simple; so the elements are non-evident. Of non-evident things, some are bodies, like atoms and masses, and others incorporeal, like shapes, forms and numbers. Of these, bodies are composite, made up of length, breadth, depth and resistance, or weight as well. So the elements are not only non-evident but incorporeal too."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-727a841897c74f58a403f1fd956b183e","ordinal":653,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.153","text":"\n\nMoreover, each incorporeal thing, when considered, is seen to have number belonging to it, for it is one, or two, or more. From this they conclude that the elements of the things that exist are the numbers, which are non-evident and incorporeal and observed in everything. And not numbers in general, but the unit and the indefinite dyad, which comes about through the addition of the unit, and by participation in which the particular dyads are dyads."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-2343e88f500c4ca998d92e0cd1d1fcbf","ordinal":654,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.154","text":"\n\nFor from these, they say, the other numbers come about, those observed in the things counted, and the cosmos is constructed. The point stands for the unit; the line for the dyad, since it is observed between two points; the surface for the triad, since they say it is a flowing of the line breadthwise toward another point lying to the side; and the body for the tetrad, since it comes about when the surface rises up toward a point lying above."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-15297e94c71d4e7cad45f2c0cf1ab0e9","ordinal":655,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.155","text":"\n\nIn this way they picture bodies and the whole cosmos, and they also say that the cosmos is governed according to harmonic ratios: the fourth, which is the ratio of four to three, as eight is to six; the fifth, which is the ratio of three to two, as nine is to six; and the octave, which is double, as twelve is to six."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-79d98351506149da95d2508762630a23","ordinal":656,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.156","text":"\n\nThese are their dreams. They also try to establish that number is something other than the things counted. If an animal, by its own definition, is one, say, then a plant, since it is not an animal, will not be one; but ⟨the⟩ plant is one too. So an animal is not one ⟨as an animal⟩, but by virtue of something else, observed outside it, in which each thing participates and through which it becomes one. And if number is the things counted, then, since the things counted are, say, people and cattle and horses, number will be people and cattle and horses; and number will be white and black and bearded, if the things measured happen to be like that."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-04f438f1a1fa452fb833bae811626a59","ordinal":657,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.157","text":"\n\nBut these consequences are absurd. So number is not the things counted, but has a reality of its own apart from them, by virtue of which it is observed in the things counted and is an element. When they had concluded in this way that number is not the things counted, the puzzle against number crept in. For it is said that if there is number, either number is the things counted themselves or something else outside them. But number is not the things counted themselves, as the Pythagoreans proved, nor something else besides them, as we shall point out. So number is nothing."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-53f64f68d3ed42da826b8fe75babc55b","ordinal":658,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.158","text":"\n\nThat number is not something outside, besides the things counted, we shall show by resting the argument on the unit, for clarity of exposition. If the unit is something by itself, by participating in which each of the things that participate in it becomes one, either this unit will be one, or there will be as many units as there are things that participate in it. If it is one, does each of the things said to participate in it participate in the whole of it or in a part of it? If, say, the one human being has the whole unit, there will no longer be a unit for the one horse or the one dog to participate in, or for each of the other things that we call one. It is just as if we supposed that there were many naked people"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-e20bfbdcf6f3418f86a47a6fe35beb69","ordinal":659,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.159","text":"\n\nand only one cloak, and one of them put it on: the rest will remain naked and without a cloak. And if each participates in a part of it, then first, the unit will have some part, and indeed infinitely many parts into which it is divided, which is absurd. Next, just as a part of the ten, such as the two, is not a ten, so a part of the unit will not be a unit either; and for this reason nothing will participate in the unit. So the unit in which particular things are said to participate is not one."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-3c488b4a017f48d99f3731b6aa020086","ordinal":660,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.160","text":"\n\nSuppose instead that the units, by participation in which each particular thing is called one, are equal in number to the things counted that are called one. Then the units participated in will be infinite. And these units either participate in a higher unit, or in units equal in number to themselves (and that is why they are units), or they do not participate but are units without any participation."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-c80ded7c42a54e9da73a549350f55a78","ordinal":661,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.161","text":"\n\n⟨...⟩ For if they can be units without participation, each perceptible thing too will be able to be one without participation in a unit, and the unit said to be observed by itself is overturned outright. And if those units too are units by participation, either they all participate in one unit, or each participates in its own. If they all participate in one, each will be said to participate either in a part of it or in the whole of it, and the original absurdities remain."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-53d7232aad7f4356bd4efacf8a1e4fea","ordinal":662,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.162","text":"\n\nAnd if each participates in its own, a unit must be observed over each of those units too, and others over those, and so on to infinity. To apprehend that there are some units by themselves, by participation in which each existing thing is one, we would have to have apprehended infinitely many infinities of intelligible units. But it is impossible to apprehend infinitely many infinities of intelligible units. So it is impossible to declare that there are intelligible units and that each existing thing is one by becoming one through participation in its own unit."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-a16bda7d93c74ce09182eb55e4b1c7ae","ordinal":663,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.163","text":"\n\nSo it is absurd to say that there are as many units as there are things that participate in them. And if the unit said to exist by itself is neither one nor as many as the things that participate in it, there is no unit by itself at all. Likewise none of the other numbers will exist by itself either, since the argument just put forward about the unit, as an example, can be used for all the numbers. But if number neither exists by itself, as we have pointed out, nor is the things counted themselves, as the Pythagoreans showed, and there is nothing besides these, we must say that number does not exist either."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-7a9f133b19ab4ac4a7af1996b70f150c","ordinal":664,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.164","text":"\n\nAnd how do those who think that number is something outside, besides the things counted, say that the dyad comes into being from the unit? When we put a unit together with another unit, either something is added to the units from outside, or something is subtracted from them, or nothing is either added or subtracted. If nothing is either added or subtracted, there will be no dyad. For the units, when apart from each other, did not have the dyad observed in them by their own definition, and nothing has now been added to them from outside,"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-5f3ea42f1b334c9c80d8058b30f5c778","ordinal":665,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.165","text":"\n\njust as nothing has been subtracted either, by hypothesis. So the putting together of a unit with a unit will not be a dyad, if there is neither subtraction nor addition of anything from outside. If subtraction takes place, not only will there be no dyad, but the units will actually be diminished. And if the dyad is added to them from outside so that a dyad may come about from the units, what seem to be two will be four. For there is a unit and another unit to begin with, and if a dyad were added to them from outside, the number four would result."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-8c2ff49c3a2640a996fdd5885cfa254a","ordinal":666,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.166","text":"\n\nThe same argument applies to the other numbers that are said to be brought about by composition. If, then, the numbers said to be composite come about from the higher ones neither by subtraction nor by addition nor without subtraction and addition, the coming into being of a number said to exist on its own, apart from the things counted, does not hold together. That composite numbers are not ungenerated either, the dogmatists themselves make plain, since they say that these are composed and come into being from the higher ones, that is, the unit and the indefinite dyad."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-e66eb7649c5d4b2d9bd0764e318bd0fc","ordinal":667,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.167","text":"\n\nSo number does not exist on its own. And if number is neither observed on its own nor has its reality in the things counted, then number is not anything, as far as the over-subtleties introduced by the dogmatists go. Let this much be said about the so-called physical part of philosophy too, which is enough for an outline."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-b97b663a7e834272ab70d5f625d50f8e","ordinal":668,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.168","text":"\n\nThere remains the ethical part, which seems to be concerned with distinguishing fine, bad and indifferent things. So, to treat this too in summary fashion, let us investigate the existence of good, bad and indifferent things, first setting out the concept of each."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-dc6ac221ca1e41cf96f69934e383a2e3","ordinal":669,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.169","text":"\n\nThe Stoics say that good is benefit or not other than benefit. By benefit they mean excellence and excellent action; by what is not other than benefit, the excellent person and the friend. For excellence, being the ruling part in a certain condition, and excellent action, being an activity in accordance with excellence, are benefit outright, while the excellent person and the friend are not other than benefit."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-a105052df2234351bcb82a5254306115","ordinal":670,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.170","text":"\n\nFor benefit is a part of the excellent person, being his ruling part. Wholes, they say, are neither the same as their parts (for a man is not a hand) nor other than their parts (for they do not exist without the parts). That is why they say wholes are not other than their parts. Hence they say that the excellent person, being a whole in relation to his own ruling part, which they called benefit, is not other than benefit. From this they go on to say that the good is spoken of in three ways."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-7a3d348f3cb743cfb2d86ecffaef4b8e","ordinal":671,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.171","text":"\n\nIn one way, they say, good is that by which one can be benefited, and this is the most fundamental good, namely excellence. In another, it is that according to which benefit comes about, such as excellence and actions in accordance with excellence. In a third way, it is what is able to benefit; and this is excellence, ⟨the⟩ action in accordance with excellence, the excellent person and the friend, and gods and excellent divinities. So the second sense of good includes the first, and the third includes the second and the first."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-94082cf2c13946b6a18aef364ce99a7d","ordinal":672,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.172","text":"\n\nSome say that good is what is choiceworthy for its own sake; others, what contributes to happiness or helps make it up. Happiness, as the Stoics say, is a smooth flow of life. These, roughly, are the things said about the concept of the good."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-f78fd94a6c574be288acb7993da8c1fc","ordinal":673,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.173","text":"\n\nBut whether one says that good is what benefits, or what is choiceworthy for its own sake, or what contributes to happiness, one does not show what the good is, but states one of its accidents. That is futile. For the properties just mentioned belong either to the good alone or to other things as well. If they belong to other things as well, they are not distinctive marks of the good, since they are shared. If they belong to the good alone, we cannot conceive the good from them."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-1ac9c04e547b4a4ba257ddb1dc8a3c2d","ordinal":674,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.174","text":"\n\nSomeone who has no concept of a horse does not know what neighing is, and cannot arrive at a concept of a horse through it, unless he has first come across a horse neighing. In the same way, someone who is inquiring what the good is because he does not know the good cannot recognize what belongs peculiarly to it alone, so as to be able to conceive the good itself through that. For one must first learn the nature of the good itself, and only then understand that it benefits, that it is choiceworthy for its own sake, and that it produces happiness."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-ebf0e7b32d9642248808a953da512c66","ordinal":675,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.175","text":"\n\nThat the accidents just mentioned are not enough to reveal the concept and nature of the good, the dogmatists show in practice. Everyone, perhaps, agrees that the good benefits, that it is choiceworthy (which is why it is called good, agathon, as if it were agaston, “admirable”), and that it produces happiness. But when they are asked what it is that these properties belong to, they fall into a war without truce: some say excellence, some pleasure, some freedom from pain, some something else. Yet if what the good itself is had been shown by the definitions given above, they would not be at odds, as though its nature were unknown."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-9a8efa5dfd6241079533f366024af9d4","ordinal":676,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.176","text":"\n\nThis, then, is how those reputed to be the most eminent of the dogmatists differ about the concept of the good. They have differed in the same way about the bad, some saying that bad is harm or not other than harm, others that it is what is to be avoided for its own sake, others that it is what produces unhappiness. In saying these things they state not the essence of the bad but, perhaps, some of its accidents, and so they fall into the difficulty described above."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-9ff65e94d7c44cd4a794b76d797f5f0d","ordinal":677,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.177","text":"\n\nIndifferent, they say, is spoken of in three ways. In one way, it is that toward which there is neither impulse nor repulsion, such as the number of the stars, or of the hairs on one’s head, being even. In another, it is that toward which there is impulse or repulsion, but no more toward this than toward that, as with two indistinguishable four-drachma coins when one has to choose one of them: there is an impulse to choose one of them, but no more toward this one than toward that. In a third way, they say, indifferent is what contributes neither to happiness nor to unhappiness, such as health and wealth; for what can be used sometimes well and sometimes badly is, they say, indifferent, and it is mainly this sense, they say, that they discuss in ethics."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-564dac4fa11345bd98082ebe5a7d0a78","ordinal":678,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.178","text":"\n\nWhat one should think about this concept too is clear from what we have said about good and bad things. So it is plain that they have not brought us to the concept of each of the things mentioned. Nor is it surprising that this has happened to them, since they are stumbling over things that perhaps have no reality. For some reason as follows to the conclusion that nothing is by nature good or bad or indifferent."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-dc4400e872674ae4bb908ed7dfccfc02","ordinal":679,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.179","text":"\n\nFire, which heats by nature, appears heating to everyone; snow, which cools by nature, appears cooling to everyone; and everything that affects us by nature affects alike everyone who is, as they say, in a natural condition. But none of the so-called goods affects everyone as good, as we shall point out. So there is nothing good by nature. That none of the so-called goods affects everyone alike is clear, they say."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-37d1b6e3a2bc4cc994b2f4134ce1a334","ordinal":680,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.180","text":"\n\nLet us pass over ordinary people, some of whom think that bodily fitness is good, others sexual indulgence, others gluttony, others heavy drinking, others gambling, others greed, and others things even worse than these. Among the philosophers themselves, some say that there are three kinds of goods, like the Peripatetics: some goods, they say, are in the soul, like the excellences; some concern the body, like health and the like; and some are external, like friends, wealth"}]}